\begin{frame}{Why neural networks belong in a linear algebra course}
Modern machine learning and artificial intelligence are often presented through the lens of multivariable calculus and black-box software libraries
\vfill
At their structural core, however, neural networks are \defword{compositions of matrix operators} interleaved with simple coordinate-wise non-linearities
\vfill
In this unit, we unify the major threads of the course:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Affine maps and hyperplanes} (Unit 06): a single neuron is an affine functional followed by a threshold or activation function
\item \textbf{Matrix multiplication and rank bounds}: forward propagation is batch matrix multiplication and non-linearity prevents rank collapse
\item \textbf{SVD and PCA} (Units 04 and 05): linear autoencoders learn orthogonal projections onto principal subspaces
\item \textbf{Graph matrices and Markov chains} (Units 08 to 10): graph convolutional networks combine left-multiplication by normalised adjacency matrices with right-multiplication by weight matrices
\end{itemize}
\end{frame}


